    "result_text": "The full suite is green (BUILD SUCCESS, 0 failures).\n\n## Summary\n\nI added a rule to `POST /api/owners` that rejects a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server date with a 400, following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions.\n\n**New files:**\n- `EnsureRegistrationDateNotFuture.java` \u2014 a single-responsibility pipeline step that reads the validated `@Val OwnerFieldsDto` and throws when the supplied date is after `LocalDate.now()`. Omitted dates pass through (they still default to the server date in the existing `ResolveOwnerRegistrationDate`).\n- `FutureRegistrationDateException.java` \u2014 the checked escalation exception.\n- `FutureRegistrationDateExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds `400` with `FieldErrorsResponse([\"registrationDate\"])`, matching the existing `InvalidPostcode`/`InvalidTelephone` handlers.\n- `...FutureRegistrationDateException.yml` \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\n**Wiring:** inserted an `ensureRegistrationDate` step into `owners.POST.yml` right after `validate` (so the body is already published) and before `ensurePostcode`, keeping it in the early 400-validation group and under the same transaction governance.\n\nI deliberately kept this out of `ResolveOwnerRegistrationDate` \u2014 that class resolves the default and applies the business-day roll; the future-date rejection is a distinct input-validation concern, so it lives in its own step and handler.",
